/* data/learn-c/phy-12-01.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-01.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-01"] = {"1.1":{"s":"This chapter is about things that go round. Revolution means moving in a circle around a point outside the body, like the Moon around the Earth. Rotation means turning about an axis through the body itself, like a spinning fan.","w":["Revolution is about an axis outside the body; rotation is about an axis through the body.","A point on a rotating body moves in a circle, but the body as a whole is not just a particle."],"x":["Two words are easy to mix up. Revolution means a body goes around a point or axis that is outside it. The Earth revolves around the Sun, and a stone tied to a string revolves around your hand. Rotation means a body turns about an axis that passes through it. A spinning fan, a top and the Earth turning once a day are all rotation.","A body can do both at the same time. The Earth rotates about its own axis once a day and revolves around the Sun once a year.","This chapter first studies motion in a circle (the speed, the force that bends the path, banked roads and vertical circles). Then it studies how solid bodies turn: moment of inertia, torque, angular momentum and rolling."],"e":"A child on a merry-go-round rotates with it, and the child also revolves around the centre of the ride. If the child stands at the very centre, there is rotation but almost no revolution."},"1.2":{"s":"Circular motion is always accelerated, because the direction of velocity keeps changing. It is also periodic. We describe it with angular displacement, angular velocity (ω) and angular acceleration, the rotational partners of s, v and a. Linear speed and angular speed are linked by v = ωr. In uniform circular motion the speed is constant and the acceleration points to the centre: a = v²/r = ω²r.","f":[{"x":"v = omega*r","n":"Linear speed from angular speed; r = radius of the circle.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.2, p.2"},{"x":"F = m*v^2/r = m*r*omega^2","n":"Size of the centripetal force for uniform circular motion.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.2, p.3"}],"w":["Centripetal force is not a new kind of force; it is the resultant of the real forces, pointing to the centre.","Centrifugal force appears only in the rotating frame; never add both in one equation."],"x":["In circular motion the direction of the velocity changes at every instant, even if the speed stays the same. A change in velocity means acceleration, so circular motion is always accelerated motion. That is why a force must act on the body all the time.","To describe the turning we use angles. Angular displacement is the angle turned, in radians. Angular velocity ω is the angle turned per second. Angular acceleration α is the rate of change of ω. They match s, v and a of straight-line motion.","The link between the two descriptions is v = ωr, where r is the radius. A point farther from the centre moves faster, though all points of a turning body share the same ω.","In uniform circular motion the speed is constant. The acceleration points towards the centre and has size a = v²/r = ω²r. It is called the centripetal acceleration, and the force that causes it is the centripetal force F = mv²/r = mω²r. It is not a new kind of force: it is the name for whatever force (tension, friction, gravity, a normal force) points to the centre."],"e":"A stone of mass 0.2 kg whirls on a 0.5 m string at 4 rad/s. Its speed is v = ωr = 2 m/s. The tension supplies F = mω²r = 0.2 × 16 × 0.5 = 1.6 N towards your hand."},"1.3":{"s":"Uses of uniform circular motion. On a flat curved road, friction alone gives the centripetal force, so there is a safe speed limit. On a banked road the tilt of the road helps; the best speed with no friction needs tan θ = v²/(rg). A two-wheeler rider leans inward for the same reason. A conical pendulum and the 'well of death' are other examples.","f":[{"x":"v_max = sqrt(mu_s*r*g)","n":"Flat curved road: highest speed before skidding; mu_s = coefficient of static friction.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.3, p.4"},{"x":"v_min = sqrt(r*g/mu_s)","n":"Well of death: lowest speed that stops the rider sliding down.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.3, p.5"},{"x":"tan(theta) = v^2/(r*g)","n":"Banked road, no friction needed: the most safe speed v_s = sqrt(r*g*tan(theta)).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.3, p.6"},{"x":"v_max = sqrt(r*g*(tan(theta) + mu_s)/(1 - mu_s*tan(theta)))","n":"Banked road with friction, upper limit; valid while mu_s*tan(theta) < 1. Lower limit: swap the signs; v_min = 0 if mu_s >= tan(theta).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.3, p.7"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(L*cos(theta)/g)","n":"Conical pendulum: period; L = string length, theta = angle of the string with the vertical.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.3, p.8"}],"w":["Mass cancels: the safe speed of a curve does not depend on the vehicle's mass.","The string of a conical pendulum can never be exactly horizontal."],"x":["On a flat road, a car turning a bend needs a centripetal force. Only the friction between the tyres and the road can supply it, so the largest safe speed satisfies mv²/r = μmg, which gives v = √(μrg). The mass cancels. Drive faster and the car skids outwards, because friction cannot give more than μmg.","Roads on sharp bends are banked, with the outer edge higher. Now a part of the normal force from the road points towards the centre and helps. For a banking angle θ and no friction, the ideal speed is v = √(rg tanθ). Two equations give this: N cosθ = mg (vertical balance) and N sinθ = mv²/r (towards the centre). Divide them and the mass and N disappear, leaving tanθ = v²/(rg).","At exactly this speed the car needs no friction at all. A little faster or slower is handled by friction.","The same idea works for a conical pendulum, a stone whirled in a horizontal circle on a string that makes angle θ with the vertical. Here T cosθ = mg and T sinθ = mω²(L sinθ), so its period is T = 2π√(L cosθ/g). Cyclists and aircraft lean into a turn for the same reason."],"e":"A bend has radius 50 m and g = 10 m/s². For tanθ = 0.2 the ideal speed is v = √(50 × 10 × 0.2) = √100 = 10 m/s, that is 36 km/h."},"1.4":{"s":"Vertical circular motion, like a bob whirled on a string. Speed is not constant: gravity changes kinetic energy into potential energy and back. The string is slackest at the top. To just complete the circle, the speed at the top must be at least √(rg), which means at least √(5rg) at the bottom.","f":[{"x":"v_top = sqrt(r*g)","n":"Mass on a string: least speed at the top to complete the circle (tension just zero).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.4, p.10"},{"x":"v_bottom = sqrt(5*r*g)","n":"Matching least speed at the lowest point.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.4, p.10"},{"x":"T_bottom - T_top = 6*m*g","n":"Difference in string tension between the lowest and highest points.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.4, p.10"},{"x":"v_max = sqrt(g*r)","n":"Convex over-bridge: above this speed the vehicle loses contact at the top (N = 0).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.4, p.11"}],"w":["A rod (not a string) can push, so the least speed at the top is about zero and 2√(rg) at the bottom.","Speed changes around a vertical circle, so use energy conservation, not v = constant."],"x":["When a bob is whirled in a vertical circle, gravity does work as it goes up and down, so the speed is not constant. It is largest at the bottom and smallest at the top.","Take a bob of mass m on a string of length r. At the top, the string pulls down and gravity also pulls down, so T + mg = mv²/r. The least speed for the string to stay tight is when T = 0, giving v_top = √(gr). At lower speeds the string goes slack and the bob leaves the circle.","Use energy conservation from the top to the bottom: the bob falls a height 2r, so ½mv_bottom² = ½mv_top² + mg(2r). With v_top² = gr, this gives v_bottom² = 5gr. So the least speed at the bottom to complete the circle is √(5gr).","The tension is largest at the bottom. At the bottom, T − mg = mv²/r, so with v² = 5gr the tension there is 6mg, six times the weight. The same ideas apply to a roller coaster loop (with a normal force in place of the tension) and to a stone whirled in a bucket of water: the water stays in at the top if v² ≥ gr."],"e":"A bob on a 1.6 m string needs v_bottom = √(5 × 10 × 1.6) = √80 = 8.94 m/s at the bottom (g = 10 m/s²). At the top it moves at √(10 × 1.6) = 4 m/s."},"1.5":{"s":"In rotation, moment of inertia (I) plays the part that mass plays in straight-line motion. It tells how hard it is to change an object's rotation. It depends on the mass and on how far that mass is from the axis. For particles, I = Σ m r². Rotational kinetic energy is ½ I ω².","f":[{"x":"I = Sigma m_i*r_i^2","n":"Moment of inertia of a set of particles; r_i = perpendicular distance from the axis.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.5, p.13"},{"x":"KE = (1/2)*I*omega^2","n":"Rotational kinetic energy.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.5, p.13"},{"x":"I = M*R^2","n":"Thin ring about its central axis.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.5, p.13"},{"x":"I = (1/2)*M*R^2","n":"Uniform disc about its central axis.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.5, p.14"}],"w":["Moment of inertia always belongs to a stated axis; the same body has a different I about a different axis."],"x":["In a straight line, mass measures how hard it is to change the velocity. In rotation, the moment of inertia I plays that role: it measures how hard it is to change the rate of turning of a body about a given axis.","I is defined as the sum of m r² over all the little masses that make up the body, where r is the distance of each from the axis. Mass far from the axis counts much more than mass near it, because r is squared.","So I depends on the total mass, on how the mass is spread out, and on which axis you choose. It does not depend on how fast the body is turning. Its unit is kg m².","Some standard values, for mass M and radius R: thin ring about its centre axis MR²; solid disc about its centre axis ½MR²; solid sphere about a diameter (2/5)MR²; thin spherical shell about a diameter (2/3)MR²; thin rod of length L about its centre perpendicular to it ML²/12, and about one end ML²/3.","This is why a flywheel has most of its mass in the rim. A large I makes the speed change slowly, which smooths out the jerks of an engine."],"e":"A ring and a disc have the same mass 2 kg and radius 0.5 m. For the ring I = MR² = 0.5 kg m², and for the disc I = ½MR² = 0.25 kg m². The ring is twice as hard to spin up."},"1.6":{"s":"Radius of gyration (K) is the distance from the axis at which the whole mass could be placed to give the same moment of inertia. So I = M K². A larger K means the mass is spread farther from the axis.","f":[{"x":"I = M*K^2","n":"K = radius of gyration.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.6, p.14"},{"x":"K = R/sqrt(2)","n":"Disc about its central axis; for a ring K = R.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.6, p.14"}],"w":["For the same M and R, a ring has a larger K (and I) than a disc: its mass is all at the rim."],"x":["Radius of gyration K is a way to describe the spread of mass with a single length. It is the distance from the axis at which you could imagine all the mass placed, as one point, and still get the same moment of inertia. So I = MK² and K = √(I/M).","A body whose mass is mostly far from the axis has a large K. For a thin ring about its centre axis, I = MR², so K = R. For a solid disc, K = R/√2, about 0.71R. For a solid sphere about a diameter, K = R√(2/5), about 0.63R.","K is useful when comparing shapes. In rolling problems it appears as the ratio K²/R², which decides how a body rolls down a slope: the smaller K²/R² is, the faster it accelerates. You will see this again in the topic on rolling motion."],"e":"A flywheel of mass 4 kg has I = 0.16 kg m² about its axis. Its radius of gyration is K = √(0.16/4) = √0.04 = 0.2 m."},"1.7":{"s":"Two shortcuts for moment of inertia about a new axis. Parallel axes: I = I(cm) + M h², where h is the distance from the axis through the centre of mass. Perpendicular axes (flat objects only): I(z) = I(x) + I(y). Here x and y lie in the plane, z is perpendicular to it, and all three meet at one point.","f":[{"x":"I_O = I_C + M*h^2","n":"Parallel axes: I_C about the parallel axis through the centre of mass; h = distance between the axes.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.7, p.15"},{"x":"I_z = I_x + I_y","n":"Perpendicular axes: flat (laminar) bodies only; x, y in the plane, z perpendicular, all meeting at one point.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.7, p.16"}],"w":["Parallel axes needs one axis through the centre of mass; it cannot jump between two other axes directly.","Perpendicular axes does not work for a solid sphere or cylinder: only for flat bodies."],"x":["I is easy to find for an axis through the centre of mass. These two theorems let you find it for other axes.","Parallel axis theorem: if the axis is moved parallel to itself, a distance h away from the axis through the centre of mass, then I = I_cm + Mh². The extra term is always positive, so the centre-of-mass axis always gives the smallest I. Example use: a rod about its end is ML²/12 + M(L/2)² = ML²/3, and a disc about a point on its rim (axis perpendicular to the plane) is ½MR² + MR² = (3/2)MR².","Perpendicular axis theorem: for a flat body (a lamina), take two perpendicular axes x and y in its plane and a z axis through the same point perpendicular to the plane. Then I_z = I_x + I_y. It works only for flat bodies, not for solid shapes like a sphere or a cube.","Use it for a ring: about the axis through the centre perpendicular to the plane, I_z = MR². By symmetry I_x = I_y, so each is ½MR², which is the value about a diameter of the ring. For a disc, I_z = ½MR² and each diameter gives ¼MR²."],"e":"A disc of mass 2 kg and radius 0.2 m. About its centre axis, I = ½ × 2 × 0.04 = 0.04 kg m². About a parallel axis through the rim: I = 0.04 + 2 × 0.2² = 0.04 + 0.08 = 0.12 kg m²."},"1.8":{"s":"Angular momentum is the moment of linear momentum about an axis: L = r × p. For a rigid body turning about a fixed axis, L = I ω. It is the rotational partner of linear momentum p = m v.","f":[{"x":"L = r*p*sin(theta)","n":"Angular momentum of a particle; theta = angle between r and p.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.8, p.17"},{"x":"L = I*omega","n":"Rigid body about a fixed axis.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.8, p.17"}],"w":["Angular momentum depends on the axis chosen. Always state which axis you are using.","L = I ω is for a rigid body about a fixed axis; for a particle use the cross product of r and p."],"x":["In straight-line motion, momentum p = mv. Its turning partner is angular momentum. For a single particle, L = r × p, which has size r p sinθ (the moment of its momentum about the point). For a particle in a circle, L = mvr = mr²ω.","For a rigid body turning about a fixed axis, add up all the particles and you get L = Iω. The direction is along the axis, given by the right-hand rule: curl the fingers of the right hand in the direction of the turn and the thumb points along L.","The unit of L is kg m²/s. The most important fact is the link with torque: the rate of change of angular momentum equals the net external torque, τ = dL/dt. This is Newton's second law in its rotational form, and it works even when I changes.","A body in uniform circular motion has L = mvr constant in size and direction, because the centripetal force passes through the centre and gives no torque."],"e":"A wheel with I = 0.5 kg m² turns at 20 rad/s. Its angular momentum is L = Iω = 10 kg m²/s along the axis."},"1.9":{"s":"Torque is the rotational partner of force. For a rigid body about a fixed axis, torque = I α, where α is the angular acceleration, just as F = m a. Torque also equals the rate of change of angular momentum.","f":[{"x":"tau = I*alpha","n":"Torque about a fixed axis; alpha = angular acceleration (like F = m*a).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.9, p.17"}],"w":["Torque equals I times alpha only about a fixed axis through the body. The moment of inertia must be about that same axis.","Torque and work have the same unit but they are different quantities."],"x":["A force can turn a body only if it does not pass through the axis. Torque measures this turning effect. It is the force times the perpendicular distance from the axis to the line of the force: τ = rF sinθ, where θ is the angle between the arm r and the force F. It is largest when the force is at right angles to the arm.","That is why a door handle is far from the hinge, and why a long spanner loosens a tight nut more easily.","For a rigid body turning about a fixed axis, the net torque causes an angular acceleration α in the way a force causes an acceleration: τ = Iα. This comes from τ = dL/dt with L = Iω and I constant. A bigger I means a smaller α for the same torque.","Work and power have rotational forms too. A torque τ turning a body by angle θ does work W = τθ, and the power is P = τω. The unit of torque is N m, but it is not a joule here, because a torque is a different quantity from energy."],"e":"A torque of 12 N m acts on a wheel with I = 3 kg m² from rest. α = τ/I = 4 rad/s², so after 5 s it turns at ω = αt = 20 rad/s."},"1.10":{"s":"If no external torque acts, angular momentum stays constant: I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂. A skater pulling in her arms makes I smaller, so she spins faster. A diver tucks in to turn faster in the air.","f":[{"x":"tau = dL/dt","n":"Torque is the rate of change of angular momentum.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.10, p.18"},{"x":"I_1*omega_1 = I_2*omega_2","n":"No external torque: angular momentum stays the same.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.10, p.19"}],"w":["When I falls, ω rises, but the rotational KE = L²/(2I) rises too: the body does work pulling inward."],"x":["Because τ = dL/dt, if the net external torque on a system is zero, its angular momentum cannot change: I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂. This is a conservation law, as important as the conservation of linear momentum.","When I gets smaller, ω must get larger. A spinning skater pulls her arms in. Her I falls, so she spins faster. Her kinetic energy goes up, too, because her muscles do work to pull the arms in. A diver curls into a ball to rotate quickly in the air and stretches out to slow the spin before entering the water.","When two bodies on the same axis join, as when a clutch connects two discs, the total angular momentum before and after is the same. The final ω is lower, and some kinetic energy is lost as heat.","A planet moving around the Sun is another case: the pull of the Sun passes through the Sun's centre, so no torque acts, and the planet's angular momentum is constant. That is why it moves faster when it is closer to the Sun."],"e":"A skater has I = 4 kg m² and ω = 2 rad/s. She pulls in her arms so that I = 1.6 kg m². Then ω = (4 × 2)/1.6 = 5 rad/s."},"1.11":{"s":"Rolling is rotation and straight-line motion together. Total kinetic energy = ½ m v² + ½ I ω², which can be written ½ m v² (1 + K²/R²). Let objects roll down a slope from rest. The one with the smaller K²/R² is faster at the bottom and reaches first, so a solid sphere beats a ring.","f":[{"x":"E = (1/2)*M*v^2*(1 + K^2/R^2)","n":"Total kinetic energy of a rolling body (translation + rotation).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.11, p.19"},{"x":"v = sqrt(2*g*h/(1 + K^2/R^2))","n":"Speed after rolling down a height h from rest without slipping.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.11, p.20"},{"x":"a = g*sin(theta)/(1 + K^2/R^2)","n":"Acceleration down an incline of angle theta when rolling.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.11, p.20"},{"x":"K^2/R^2 = 1, 1/2, 2/5, 2/3","n":"Ring or hollow cylinder, disc or solid cylinder, solid sphere, hollow sphere.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.11, p.20"}],"w":["Smaller K²/R² reaches the bottom first: solid sphere, then disc, then hollow sphere, then ring.","Pure rolling needs static friction, but it does no work, so energy is conserved."],"x":["Rolling is a combination of two motions: the centre of mass moves in a straight line with speed v, and the body spins about the centre of mass with angular velocity ω. When there is no slipping, the point touching the ground is momentarily at rest, so v = ωR.","The kinetic energy is the sum of the two parts: KE = ½mv² + ½Iω². With I = mK² and ω = v/R, this becomes KE = ½mv²(1 + K²/R²). For a ring (K²/R² = 1) the total is mv², half of it rotational. For a solid sphere (2/5) the rotational part is 2/7 of the total.","Now roll a body down an incline of angle θ, with no slipping. Energy conservation gives the acceleration of its centre: a = g sinθ / (1 + K²/R²). A body that does not roll (a block on a smooth slope) has a = g sinθ, so a rolling body is always slower, because some of the energy goes into spinning it.","Compare shapes of equal radius: a solid sphere (K²/R² = 2/5) beats a disc (1/2), which beats a hollow sphere (2/3), which beats a ring (1). The result does not depend on mass or radius, only on the shape."],"e":"A solid sphere rolls down a 30° slope (g = 10 m/s²). a = 10 × 0.5/(1 + 0.4) = 5/1.4 = 3.57 m/s², compared with 5 m/s² for a block sliding without friction."}};
